ainst the virus itself like a computer  code  synthetic mrna tells the cells of the  body how to make the spike protein  scientists had to find a way to inject  it without having the immune system  immediately attack it they came up with  a lipid nanoparticle  a kind of invisibility cloak a fatty  bubble that encapsulates the mrna  and keeps it from being destroyed  allowing it to slip  into the cell once inside the mrna is  released  and the cell starts building the spike  protein mimicking a covid19 infection  it is completely harmless but it  triggers the immune system  into creating antibodies against the  coronavirus  if the person gets infected those  antibodies prevent the virus from  entering the cell  by attaching themselves to the spike  proteins  that's what creates immunity adding  this additional sars cov2 spike protein  mrna in our cells is like adding a drop  of water into the ocean  the cells don't see this as anything  different from what they're already  bursting with anyway and contrary to a  myth circulating on social media  the vaccines cannot and do not alter  your genetic makeup your dna  it's not something that's capable of  changing the dna  in our cells dna makes mrna but mrna  does not  make or manipulate dna there is a  canadian connection to the creation of  the vaccine  in this vancouver lab tom madden and his  team at acuitis therapeutics  helped create one of the key ingredients  in the pfizer biontech vaccine  those lipid nanoparticles early  2020 we sort of realized the potential  threat  from covid19 and so we discussed with  biontech that a vaccine against covid19  is really something we should be working  on as quickly as possible  both companies could work off existing  technology to get the kovid 19 vaccines  out faster  that's the beauty of this is is  everything could stay the same  and you simply have a tweaked messenger  rna  that exactly encodes the new variants  nothing else changes  even before covid mrna technology was  being tested on other infectious  diseases  such as ebola zika virus and influenza  cancer cells also make proteins that can  be targeted by  mrna vaccines the world could be on the  cusp  of a new era in vaccinology i'm very  hopeful  that this is just the first example of a  messenger rna vaccine  that will have global impact and there  will be  others that also making a big  contribution to medicine in the future  i think we're going to see a massive  explosion  in mrna vaccines that are widely  available over the course of the next  five to ten years  the vaccines went through rigorous human  clinical trials in tens of thousands of  people  before they were approved some did  experience mild  short-lived side effects like pain at  the site of injection  body chills feeling tired or feverish  that is not uncommon with many vaccines  yes it has side effect but i didn't feel  anything my kids my daughter was calling  me  mommy are you okay i said yes i'm fine  ursula winston is a personal support  worker at a long-term care home in  toronto where there was an  outbreak it's a relief for me i mean  i needed to do this i wanted to do this  getting the vaccine she believes will  keep both her  and the residents safer  i made the decision because  the resident needs me they need us  as a team they don't have enough staff  then who's going to be there  so i made the choice today she's getting  her last dose  adverse reactions typically come after  the second shot  there have been some cases of adverse  reactions in people with a history of  serious allergies  health canada advises anyone with  serious allergies or health conditions  to seek medical advice you know if you  look at the rates of  of long-term adverse events associated  with the vaccine  and compare that against those same  adverse events associated with infection  what you usually see is that the vaccine  lowers your risk  relative to getting infected pregnant  women and those who were breastfeeding  did not take part in human clinical  trials  so there's no data on potential risks to  them  overall though the evidence shows the  benefits of the approved vaccines  outweigh any risks nothing is without  risk  but the serious adverse effects that  occur  as a result of vaccination occur at  extraordinarily low rates  the first question is are you okay and  then the second question  is should i get this vaccine and i  always answer with yes  sarah kahia was the first person in  alberta to get the vaccine  and she knows vaccine hesitancy is real  to be able to be the voice and to be  able to speak to people in my community  and to speak to other people  who have seen me getting this vaccine  and i'm able to answer these questions  and i'm able to show them that i'm fine  and that this vaccine is fine  she believes many people who are  hesitant including some people in black  indigenous and racialized communities  isn't because they're anti-vaxxers  but because there's distrust of  government and the health care system  after years of racism and abuse this  same government is kind of coming up to  these communities now and saying  trust us take this vaccine just believe  what we're saying right now  they have the right to ask those  questions and it's our responsibility  it's public health responsibilities the  government's responsibility to make sure  that these questions are continually  answered  and reiterated again and again  next week on the new reality i've never  had trouble paying the rent before  this year and i've lost my job you can't  get blood from a stone  housing and health are inextricably  linked you can't disconnect the two  things  i observe tenants being evicted in as  little as  60 90 seconds i'm dealing with a  corporation that doesn't see me as a  person doesn't see this as a community  they see it as a source of profit that's  the new reality for this week  i'm donna friesen thanks for joining us  [Music]  you
 

